{"run_id": "20260803T113606-hepkg", "qid": "gen-paper-0a0748c3", "repeat": 0, "system": "hepkg", "config_hash": "b29c4f777113", "git_sha": "6945b02-dirty", "question": "How many background-estimation methods does analysis 2405.18661 use?", "shape": "count", "split": "dev", "needs": ["sql"], "difficulty": "easy", "answer": {"text": "Analysis 2405.18661 uses 21 statistical methods for background estimation. Here are some examples:\n\n- 95% CL upper limit setting on Higgs boson pair production cross section and self-coupling\n- Angular weighting method using event weights functions of rapidity and cos(theta*) for acceptance-corrected AFB\n- Combined fit to MVA output distributions in categories of jet and b jet multiplicity\n- Fit to MVA discriminator output (or ancillary kinematic variables) across event categories\n- Signal-background separation via a robust kinematic variable or a multivariate-analysis (MVA) approach\n\nFor a complete list, please refer to the full set of methods listed in the paper.\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"answer\", \"arguments\": {\"text\": \"Analysis 2405.18661 uses 21 statistical methods for background estimation. Here are some examples:\\n\\n- 95% CL upper limit setting on Higgs boson pair production cross section and self-coupling\\n- Angular weighting method using event weights functions of rapidity and cos(theta*) for acceptance-corrected AFB\\n- Combined fit to MVA output distributions in categories of jet and b jet multiplicity\\n- Fit to MVA discriminator output (or ancillary kinematic variables) across event categories\\n- Signal-background separation via a robust kinematic variable or a multivariate-analysis (MVA) approach\\n\\nFor a complete list, please refer to the full set of methods listed in the paper.\", \"answerable\": true, \"reason\": \"answered\"}}}\n</tool_call>", "answered": true, "papers": [], "value": null, "steps": [{"round": 1, "tool": "contents_of", "args": {"paper_ids": ["2405.18661"], "predicate": "result_uses_s