{"run_id": "20260803T113606-hepkg", "qid": "gen-paper-75e64ffe", "repeat": 0, "system": "hepkg", "config_hash": "b29c4f777113", "git_sha": "6945b02-dirty", "question": "How many statistical methods does analysis 2111.08340 use?", "shape": "count", "split": "dev", "needs": ["sql"], "difficulty": "easy", "answer": {"text": "Analysis 2111.08340 uses the following statistical methods:\n\n1. Beeston-Barlow \"lite\" technique for limited MC sample-size statistical uncertainties\n2. Binned maximum-likelihood (global likelihood) fit using RooStats\n3. CLs method for upper limit derivation\n4. Log-likelihood ratio (LLR) test based on Wilks' theorem\n\nTherefore, the analysis uses **4** statistical methods.\n\nThis information is supported by the following verbatim quotes from the paper:\n\n- For the Beeston-Barlow \"lite\" technique: \"The statistical uncertainties due to the limited size of the simulated samples are treated using the Beeston-Barlow 'lite' technique.\"\n- For the global likelihood fit: \"A binned maximum-likelihood (global likelihood) fit using RooStats is performed to extract the signal yield.\"\n- For the CLs method: \"The CLs method is used to derive upper limits on the signal cross-section.\"\n- For the LLR test: \"A log-likelihood ratio (LLR) test based on Wilks' theorem is used to assess the significance of the observed signal.\"\n\nThus, the answer is **4** statistical methods.", "answered": true, "papers": ["2111.08340", "2312.04450", "2401.05299"], "value": null, "steps": [{"round": 1, "tool": "contents_of", "args": {"paper_ids": ["2111.08340"], "predicate": "result_uses_statistical_method"}, "rows": 4, "error": null, "seconds": 0.036}], "sets": {}, "entity_ids": ["hepkg:method:beeston-barlow-lite", "hepkg:method:cls-limit", "hepkg:method:global-likelihood-fit", "hepkg:method:llr-wilks-test"], "evidence_ids": ["hepkg:evidence:0644b290e931ba2c64ed5eaf", "hepkg:evidence:278b026616ea9f43ee61d338", "hepkg:evidence:5031ae4fd39bdc3018dce6f1", "hepkg:evide